Z||000027||EdsB52||07-17-2001||12:29 PM||||I don't see how GM can whine about not selling enough Camaros so they need to shut down the line. Driving my Fiero actually gets far more attention than diving my Z-28. While in the latter, I feel about as exclusive as Honda driver. If I drive 20 miles, I can be assured I'll see at least 5 Camaros, maybe not Z-28's, but they're all the same from the exterior except the little Z-28 emblems that are inconspicuous. GM didn't even give the Z-28's better wheels than the stock Camaros. I believe there are other politics playing into the demise of the Camaro than just sales figures.||||reg||1
Z||000028||AusFiero||07-17-2001||10:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 84Bill:<BR><B>I wonder what GM has up it's sleeve? There has to be something to replace theese cars.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This news I knew of 2 years ago, amazing what we find out in Australia when you have the right industry contacts.<P>I can assure there is a replacement due for 2005. America is going to get the new Holden Commodore Coupe that is due for release here next year. Some sheetmetal changes will occur to make it slightly different. Pontiac may not change much as it already is a split grilled Pontiac looking car anyway.<P>But on the upside the Commodore coupe will come equipped with a 300KW 5.7 ltr LS1 engine and 6 speed transmission standard. These are hot cars capable of eating Corvettes for breakfast (has already been proved in track tests here), well unless GM goes stupid and tones them down so the Corvette doesn't look wimpy by comparison. <P>Why they keep the Corvete I never can figure as it was also one of the reasons Fiero got dropped for 1989, it was getting to sporty and the GM big boys didn't want the competition in house. The Corvette is 1950's technology dragged on for nostaliga reasons only. Yes it is sporty but lets face it, they just don't handle all that good compared to their competition.<P>I will dig out some pictures of the Commodore coupe and post them when I can. It is a very very nice car and I bet it will look even better once GM USA put t-tops on it.<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000029||Songman||07-17-2001||10:33 PM||||I think most of us have known about the demise of the F-bodies for a couple of years now but never really gave it much though until it finally hit us in the face...<P>Personally, it don't bother me that these cars are leaving our midst, but I do hate to see the nameplate leave. But as in so many other cases, the later models of these cars didn't hold up to their original intentions.<P>The Corvette and Thunderbird are two other cars that didn't. Actually, the Corvette stayed closest to it's origins. <P>Glad to have you back with us, Aus!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Galen||07-17-2001||10:34 PM||||Why can't a sports car be front wheel drive? thats rather closed minded way of thinking.<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"It may be the opinion of the court, but its still wrong"</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/download.html" TARGET=_blank>Download FieroProject.com Chat Client v1.22 Today</A>||||reg||1
Z||000031||AusFiero||07-18-2001||12:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>Why can't a sports car be front wheel drive? thats rather closed minded way of thinking.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh don't get anybody wrong Galen. Front wheel drive is a very common sports car layout and some great ones have been produced over the years. It just doesn't have the handling capabilities of mid engined ones.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000032||Galen||07-18-2001||01:23 AM||||I know but mr slammed said FWD cars couldn't be called sports cars.. Mid engine cars, imo, are better handling that FWD cars.. but thats no reason for it to be said that FWD cars ARN'T true sports cars.<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"It may be the opinion of the court, but its still wrong"</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/download.html" TARGET=_blank>Download FieroProject.com Chat Client v1.22 Today</A>||||reg||1
Z||000033||Pontiaddict||07-18-2001||03:36 AM||||Orief, They try to put the GTO name on too many poorly designed things. None of which should be built much less called a GTO. Also they did downsize the F-body into a FWD,thy called them Berettas. The Nova name was put on a Japaneese econobox, and I wouldn't mind actually owning a late 80's Lemans. A friend of mine had one and although it was a crackerbox, it was kind of fun to beat on.<BR>Oh, <A HREF="http://www.thirdgen.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.thirdgen.org</A>  and <A HREF="http://www.nethirdgen.org" TARGET=_blank>http://www.nethirdgen.org</A>  are two 3rd gen oriented f-body websites. They both use the same message board program as this place does too.<P>Chadman, i know what you mean, I wish I could but that straight 6 in a 3rd gen Firebird with the 6 speed.<P>Aus, Welcome home. Glad to hear your operation went well.<BR>Commodores (the only ones I've seen are 4 doors) are kind of like Cateras here. Here though, they only come with worthless 3L v6's. It was rumored that they were gonna put LS1's in them but sell them in other countries and not in the U.S. (they are called Omegas everywhere else)<BR>They are redesigning the Catera to look more like a Cadillac and swithching model platforms. This platform would make a great base for a high performance sports coupe. which I think is going to happen. It might not be an f-body but it ought to be interesting.<P>Songman, The original intention of the Firebird nameplate was to be a 2 seat plastic bodied sports car. So it never held up to it's original intentions. The Camaro, besides being a little bigger than it used to be, still holds true to it's original concept. The Corvette hasn't since the first put big blocks in them. It's origin was closer to a Miata than what it is now. <P>Galen, well name a fwd car that you consider to be a sports car. Then list the attributes that make it one. ||||reg||1
Z||000034||Galen||07-18-2001||03:55 AM||||Dont be going against what I say, I asked someone to tell me why a FWD car couldn't be a sports car?<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"It may be the opinion of the court, but its still wrong"</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/download.html" TARGET=_blank>Download FieroProject.com Chat Client v1.22 Today</A>||||reg||1
Z||000035||p4n1c||07-18-2001||09:05 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>well name a fwd car that you consider to be a sports car. Then list the attributes that make it one. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ummm, Dodge Stealth/Mitsu 3000GT anyone?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  FWD(and a AWD version), 3.0l twin turbo V6 available with a 5 and 6(I think in the later years) speed..  320hp I believe.  Handling is okay, but not the greatest.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4<BR>*Under Restoration*<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 07-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||84Bill||07-18-2001||09:21 AM||||I was a Camaro fan for a long time and owned a 79 T Top berlinetta and 73 Coupe. I had a great time with them both, I started looking at getting a new Camaro in 98 and I noticed how bland the interior was. There just was no wow factor about the instrument panel. I happend upon a pontiac dealer and looked at a TA now there was a car with pizaz on the inside but alas I could not afford it.  So enter early 1999 when I happend to drive by a used car lot and spotted my soon to be 86 Fiero, 500 cash and the rest is history. <BR>I guess what I'm driving at here is that the Camaro needed some kick on the inside and the TA was just to expensive. My dad told me that when the Camaros and firebirds first came out the were nearly the cheapest cars to buy and you average wage earner coud get one quite easily. now they are almost luxury sport cars with a price tag to support it.||||reg||1
Z||000037||Oreif||07-18-2001||09:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B><P>The original intention of the Firebird nameplate was to be a 2 seat plastic bodied sports car. So it never held up to it's original intentions. The Camaro, besides being a little bigger than it used to be, still holds true to it's original concept. The Corvette hasn't since the first put big blocks in them. It's origin was closer to a Miata than what it is now. <P> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>According to the "Fabulous Firebird" book,<BR>The Firebird was not to be a "2-seat plastic car" It was suppose to be a 2-seat turbine engine car. When they could not get the car to work correctly they came up with a design as a 2-seat "sports car" to compete against the Corvette. But even back then Chevy had pull and GM would not authorize the car. When the mid 60's came with the muscle car craze the F-body was made. Pontiac has tried numerous times to make a 2-seater car (the "Banshee" prototypes) but Chevy and GM always seemed to stop it. The Fiero was also in "limbo" but finally when Pontiac portrayed it as a 2-seat commuter car and not a Corvette killer did GM approve it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000038||Pontiaddict||07-19-2001||01:43 AM||||The original Firebird concept made by GM was more of a testbed for turbine engine technolgy/showcar and never intended to be an actual model. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.chicagoautoshow.com/img/concepts/classic/cpt4.jpg"> <P>The '63 2 seat plastic bodied Banshee was actually intended to be a real car. But like you said, Gm put a stop to anything like it's precious Corvette.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.pontiac.com/history/images/photo_63.gif">  Notice that the car looks alot like the '64 Mako Shark Corvette and has alot of the future stingray styling cues despite being made before any of them.<P>On a side note, here's a concept car that I've never seen before, It was made [from the looks of the picture, more like computer rendered] in 1987 and looks kind of familiar.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.chicagoautoshow.com/img/concepts/classic/cpt8.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000039||LZeitgeist||07-19-2001||06:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>p4nic wrote:. Handling is okay, but not the greatest.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And that is one of the main reasons that, in my mind, FWD's aren't sports cars.<P>Sports cars are supposed to perform at least as good as they look. Sports cars are supposed to exhibit superior handling and stability - superior to all other (production) classifications of automobiles. <P>*If* this can be achieved with All-Wheel-Drive, then great - not a problem. But every Front-Wheel-Drive car I've ever driven in my life has handled like a pig. The powered front wheels constantly fighting and trying to pull the steering wheel through my hands (especially under heavy accelleration) instead of the steering pointing the car where I wanted it to go. The *removal* of weight from the powered wheels as the weight shifts back under accelleration. The weight distribution shot all to hell and feeling like an ox pulling a plastic toboggan on a rope. The premature wear of the front tires that are required to do *everything* (gripping, steering, stopping, accellerating) while the rear end adds *nothing* to the performance aspects except holding the rear end off the ground. <P>I can safely say that as long as I care about what I drive, I will *never* own a FWD car. When I get so old and feeble that all I care about is getting from Point 'A' to Point 'B', then it won't matter anymore - but until then, I like to *DRIVE*.||||reg||1
Z||000040||p4n1c||07-19-2001||09:03 AM||||I actually find my FWD to handle quite well.  I out-handled a Mercedes on a very sharp S-bend in the city.  I said the handling on the Stealth was okay, not bad.  It handles about as well as a Firebird/Camaro.  <P>By your definition though, you've ruled out another sports car, the Viper.  It's a pig in corners, the reason why it doesn't do to well in the GT races, it can beat the hell out of people in the straights but loses ground to the Corvette's and Porsche's in the corners.  <P>A friend has a '92 base model Stealth(FWD, 3.0l non-TT, I believe 180hp) and we would do e-brake turns around the islands in the middle of the streets.  I find FWD has more stability and control in rain and snow than RWD, but RWD is more fun to drive.<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 07-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||Galen||07-19-2001||09:17 AM||||my brother has a 93 ES Stealth..222hp, Handle rather nicely.. car is too big for my tastes, but I dont mind driving it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"It may be the opinion of the court, but its still wrong"</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/download.html" TARGET=_blank>Download FieroProject.com Chat Client v1.22 Today</A>||||reg||1
Z||000042||Slammed Fiero||07-19-2001||09:36 AM||||I don't want to get into a big debate , because I seem to get into those a lot.<P>Look at the original incarnations of sports cars. No not the corvette , MG's I believe were the first real sports cars, I don't know the model designations , but I believe they started in the late 30's early 40's. Small car , not much to it weight wise, rear drive are the 2 key components to a real sports car. This is not meant to put down any other cars. Espically the Stealth , Mitsu 3000GTVR4 , because there great cars, and the category the AWD version falls under is a tough one. <P>Mustang's , camaro's both fall under Pony car.<P>It seems automanufacturers are leaning away from what a sports car used to be , and should be. The vette is getting fat in it's old age and the viper was born a over weight fat kid. This is not to say they don't perform amazingly. BUT I have seen Lotus Elises beat the pants off of ZO6 vettes around a track. Why?? light weight , small displacement , rear drive (mid engine to boot)<P>We have to look what the original definition of sports car is ,before we can go throw the name on everything. I heard a kid call his Tiburon a Sports car...(haha)<P><BR>Drive a light weight car , there is no replacement for displacement?? not when it comes to sports cars ..there is no replacement for light weight. The lighter the car the more forgiving it seems to be , nimble , ect.<P>I know some of you may have the urge to pull out magazines and say look!! this car cornered at .86G's and it's not a sports car?? Gimmie some Koni's, sway bars and sticky tires I will get your moms minivan to corner at .86G's.<P>Good definition of sports car?? anything from Lotus, Colin Champman was obsessed with weight reduction. If car made it to the 2nd race without breaking , it was built too heavy.<P>NOTHING FWD!!!! <P>I talked about this last nigth with a fellow racer. He said torque steer and handling come into play too much , more power in a FWD setup = squirly handling. <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000043||LZeitgeist||07-19-2001||09:51 AM||||I've never driven a Viper, so I can't speak from experience, but in my mind, I've always seen the Viper as a Muscle Car, not a Sports Car. <P>If the Viper truly handles like a pig in the corners, then that seems to support the 'Muscle Car' classification in my mind. (Not that all Muscle Cars are pigs - but when the percentage of brute force/horsepower available is greater than the percentage of performance handling/stability, that's when the car gets pushed from the SC range into the MC range, IMHO.)<P>There's grey area all over the place in these types of classifications - some people ascribe the terms to identify appearance rather than performance, but I've always used the terms to identify the performance aspects of the car - not to say that doing otherwise is incorrect, just that I'm not in complete agreeance with those that define a Sports Car by what it looks like as opposed to how it handles under pressure.<P>Just my two cents...||||reg||1
Z||000044||p4n1c||07-19-2001||11:07 AM||||I agree that performance/handling more readily defines a sports car than looks.  In my opinion they are also defined by how conservative they are, like Slammed said, on weight.  So yes, that goes against my original statement on the Stealth and Viper being sports cars.  Firebirds, Camaros, Mustangs, etc were all born as muscle cars and they tried to mold them into a sports car. <P>I think driving a sports car is just that, a sport.  As such, like running, you want to be as streamlined and light weight as possible, but with muscles to power yourself over everyone.<P>LZ, I think the classification for the Viper as a muscle car <B>is</B> better suited for it.  That thing can rip in the straights, especially the Venom(I can't remember who did this modification to the Viper).<P>That was a total contradiction of my last message, but oh well.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000045||Fformula88||07-19-2001||09:15 PM||||Its a shame that GM let the F-bodies get to this point.  They always point to the low sales figures... and yet the last redesign appeared in '93.  I don't think there are many cars that can go 10 model years and still be selling well, even with a styling change (major for the Firebird, minor for the Chevy) in the middle.  This kind of redesign schedual puts GM to shame (ok, so Ford isn't much better since the Mustang is still built off a version of the Fairmont platform!).<P>based on most of the other GM products to appear the last few years, I'd be very scared about what would appear with one of these nameplates on it.<P>As for FWD sports cars, I think it is possible, although not easy.  The handling dynamics is different than a RWD car, but that doesn't make it bad.  I think the biggest disadvantage to them is getting the power to the ground because of weight transfer.  Under hard acceleration the weight lifts off the front wheels, making it harder to avoid wheelspin.  This puts a big limit on the amount of power you can initially put to the ground.  ||||reg||1
Z||000046||LZeitgeist||07-19-2001||09:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>As for FWD sports cars, I think it is possible, although not easy. The handling dynamics is different than a RWD car, but that doesn't make it bad. I think the biggest disadvantage to them is getting the power to the ground because of weight transfer. Under hard acceleration the weight lifts off the front wheels, making it harder to avoid wheelspin. This puts a big limit on the amount of power you can initially put to the ground.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This is why I think an All-Wheel-Drive Sports Car is definitely possible, but I don't feel a solely Front-Wheel-Drive Sports Car is truly viable. IMHO.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000047||TRiAD||07-21-2001||09:51 PM||||Technically speaking, "Sports Cars" are/were Front-engined/Rear wheel Drive, and lightweight. This term originated to describe some of the first cars in this genre from Britain. <P>I believe Colin Chapman pretty much paved the way for Mid-engined/Rear Wheel Drive cars to join them, as long as they were still lightweight. <BR>Oh, "SportsCars" are/were all open cockpit car (convertibles) as well. <P>Nowadays, Honda can make an Acura RSX that can outrun some of the early V8 Ferraris, and Lamborghini, Bugatti, Tommy Kaira and others are making/have made Mid-engined/<B>AWD</B> cars that simply must be considered exotics, if not SportsCars. The lines are definitely blurring. <P>Basically, if it's VERY limited production, and/or VERY expensive, it's an exotic;<BR>if it's FAST but heavy, it's a Gran Touring car (GT car);<BR>if it's a fast Front-engined/Rear Wheel Drive car that handles poorly, and is priced for the masses, it's a Muscle Car (Pony cars fit in this category, when equipped with the V8);<BR><B>if it's FWD, well-handling and quick, it's a "Sporty Car", not a SportsCar</B>. <P>Those are some basics that seem to be adhered to by most automotive journalists, there are exceptions, but there has been a strong attempt made to classify most every sporting car made. <P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg">  <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I><p>[This message has been edited by TRiAD (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000048||kslish||07-21-2001||10:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Good definition of sports car?? anything from Lotus, Colin Champman was obsessed with weight reduction. If car made it to the 2nd race without breaking , it was built too heavy.<P>NOTHING FWD!!!! <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Anything from Lotus?  How about the Lotus Elan M100?  It was powered by an Isuzu turbo 4 cyl, sort of Fiero-esque styling (even had fiberglass body panels), and FRONT WHEEL DRIVE.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.lotuselancentral.com/images/Gallery/MA3.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.lotuselancentral.com/images/Gallery/MA1.jpg"> <P>Here is a quote from Car & Driver "So awesome was the car’s cornering ability that exploring the chassis’s limit was almost impossible."<P>Even though the car wasn't that successful (because it was overengineered and too expensive) a lot of people still consider the Elan the best handling front wheel drive car ever and still one of the world's finest handling cars.  I'd definately call it a sports car (despite it being FWD).<P>Ken S.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000049||FieroKyle||07-21-2001||11:43 PM||||Ok, one thing, if the Grand Prix was availble in the GTP model with 2-door plus a manual transmission, defintely a sports car. If the Monte SS was offered in manual transmission, sports car too. I believe in a couple years, the Camaro and Firebird will make a triumphant return with a badder image. I think they are gonna give it a break, then return with a better style, similar with what Ford did with the T-bird. If GM wanted to really get their image up they could do this........<BR>Put a 5.7 sbc in the Impala and make it into a SS. (Blow away any Contour SVT hahaha)<BR>Keep the Firebird and Camaro.<BR>Give redesigns to both cars, bodies and performance. (better)<BR>Get rid of the Aztek, Rendevous.<BR>Make a S-10 that can beat the Ford Lighting.<BR>Keep Oldsmobile because they make high-performance Aurouras.<BR>Have a stick shift availbilty in most of its high-performance cars.<BR>Get Buick and Oldsmobile with higher performance coupes.<P>With this I believe GM will certainly attract some buyers. Otherwise I am happy with the GM line up. The Caddys are pretty strong car for the last decade. GM needs to have a couple more sports cars or high performance coupes. May be have the Monte SS with a V-8 to or the Grand Prix, or a really high performance V-6 to blow away the Eclipses which have been popular lately. But if you think, Dodge and Ford aren't better. Ford just has a Mustang and Dodge a Viper. Dodge just slaps R/T on the side of a couple of cars and gives them some extras and thats it. And Ford, their V-6 Mustang is really weak. The v-8s are pretty spunky but other than that if you don't have a T-bird special order, you don't really have much except the Mustang. And my opinion the Mustangs are ugly and have been since the 60's. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000050||FieroKyle||07-21-2001||11:47 PM||||Also, most muscle cars died when the big V-8s did however I classify the Trans Am as a muscle car.||||reg||1
